# netsurf: History API Phase B -- pushState/replaceState keep a real session stack
#
# North-star SPA milestone (doc 13 section 5 follow-up). Phase A (fixes1198)
# only updated the URL bar and history.state: pushState created no entry,
# history.length stayed 0, and back()/forward() could not return to a pushed
# URL. Now the bridge keeps a real same-document session stack in JS:
#
# - pushState() appends an entry (dropping forward entries), replaceState()
#   rewrites the current one; entry 0 is the real document load. The realm is
#   rebuilt on real navigation, so the stack never outlives its document.
# - back()/forward()/go() (native, macsurf_qjs.c) walk the JS stack first;
#   each step fires popstate with the entry's state. Past the stack's ends
#   they fall back to the native history walk (go() carries any remaining
#   delta; go(0) still reloads).
# - The physical L/R buttons (frontends/vita/input.c) consult the JS stack
#   first via macsurf_qjs_history_traverse(), then native history, then the
#   popup-close fallback, preserving the old priority order.
# - history.length is the native entry count (new __nativeLength, via
#   browser_window_history_enumerate) plus the JS entries; history.state is
#   the current entry's state.
# - location.href (hence pathname/search/hash, which parse it) reflects the
#   shown URL until a real navigation clears the override.
# - URLs resolve against the document URL with a small JS resolver (there is
#   no URL constructor in the bridge); cross-origin pushState/replaceState
#   throws SecurityError via DOMException.
#
# Test: testpages/pages/JS-history.html (30 synchronous assertions: stack
# push/back/forward/truncation/replaceState, popstate payloads, length and
# state, location reflection, SecurityError, go(-1)/go(2)), gated over HTTP
# and HTTPS in tools/run-vita-sim.sh.
diff --git a/content/handlers/javascript/quickjs/macsurf_qjs.c b/content/handlers/javascript/quickjs/macsurf_qjs.c
index a1046cb..3153860 100644
